
MetKnight - Introduction (Mini Points)

1. The Problem
Vulnerable dependencies often discovered after code is pushed.
Dependabot alerts appear in GitHub Security tab, not while coding.
SonarLint/Veracode check source code, not dependency CVEs.
Developers usually learn about issues during PRs or later security scans.
Late discovery = higher remediation cost and lower adoption.

2. What MetKnight Is
VS Code extension for real-time dependency CVE scanning.
Detects vulnerabilities directly in the editor while coding.
Includes AI-powered "Fix All" dependency upgrades.
One-liner: Dependabot, but live in your editor, offline-capable, and self-fixing.

3. How It's Different
Dependabot: Cloud-based, post-push.
SonarLint/Veracode: SAST, code-focused.
MetKnight: Live in-editor dependency scanning.
Scans as you type and on demand.
One-click AI remediation.
Works offline; no data leaves the network by default.

4. Key Pillars
Real-time: Inline warnings, hover cards, instant feedback.
Offline & Secure: Uses local GitHub Advisory Database mirror.
AI Remediation: "Fix All with Copilot" updates vulnerable packages automatically.
Governance: n-2 policy, blocklists, minimum-version rules.
Multi-language: Java, JavaScript/TypeScript, and Python.

5. Validation
Results matched real Dependabot alerts 1:1 on a MetLife repo.
Refined through direct engineering feedback and testing.

6. Demo Transition
