MetKnight - Live Demo (Mini Points)

Pre-Demo Setup
Open VS Code with a repo containing known vulnerable dependencies.
Verify GitHub Copilot Chat is signed in and working.
Run Update Advisory Database to warm the offline cache.
Close old vulnerability tabs.
Keep a backup vulnerable project ready.

1. Inline Diagnostics
Open package.json, build.gradle, pom.xml, or requirements.txt.

Highlight vulnerability squiggles/colors:
● Critical
○ High
○ Moderate
○ Low

Hover to show:
- Severity
- CVE/GHSA ID
- Summary
- Required version
- Staleness info (if enabled)

Key point: Automatic scanning as soon as the file opens.

2. Sidebar Summary
Click MetKnight in Activity Bar.
Show vulnerability counts by file and severity.

3. Vulnerability Dashboard
Open MetKnight: Show All Vulnerabilities.

Demonstrate:
- Sortable columns
- Live filtering
- Combined duplicate CVEs
- Row checkboxes for scope selection

Key point: Central view for the entire repository.

4. Fix All with Copilot (Star Feature)
Select vulnerabilities.
Click Fix All with Copilot.

Explain:
- Builds a remediation prompt.
- Opens Copilot Agent mode.
- Waits through approvals.
- Applies version upgrades automatically.

Show:
- Progress/loading bar
- Status updates (☑ Fixed / ⚠ Unresolved / 💡 Suggestion)
- Auto-rescan after completion

Fallback:
Use Command Palette → Fix All with Copilot.

5. Detailed Report
Click ⬇ export/report button.
Opens full per-CVE HTML report.
Useful for audits and non-VS Code users.

6. Governance Features
n-2 Staleness Policy: Flags outdated dependencies.

Policy-as-Code:
- Block pre-releases
- Blocklisted packages
- Minimum version requirements
- Admin-controlled configuration

7. Settings & Model Selection
Open MetKnight: Open Settings.
Show available Copilot models.

Mention:
- Offline mode is default.
- Local GHSA mirror.
- Incremental updates only.
- Optional online mode for OSV.dev lookups.

8. Wrap-Up

"Same detection as GitHub Dependabot, but live in the editor, works offline, and can fix vulnerabilities with a single click."

Quick Q&A

Q: Does code leave the machine?
A: No in offline mode; online mode only sends package name/version to OSV.dev.

Q: Supported languages?
A: Java (Gradle/Maven), JavaScript/TypeScript (npm), Python (pip).

Q: Can Copilot remove dependencies?
A: No. It only upgrades versions or flags unresolved cases.

Q: Who enables staleness policies?
A: Admins via configuration.

Q: Does it match Dependabot results?
A: Yes, validated against real Dependabot findings with 1:1 matching results.